  "message": "Separate the per-key offset by corpus, and state the limit it implies\n\nThe previous note measured the per-key offset on human text only, then quoted it\nas if it covered every population. Model output is more repetitive and its token\ndistribution more concentrated, so it has fewer effective independent draws and a\nlarger deviation from gamma for any given greenlist. Measured over 24 keys, the\noffset is sd 0.05-0.08 on human writing and 0.154 on unwatermarked model output -\nroughly three times larger.\n\nNeither changes a conclusion. On human text, the case where a false positive means\naccusing a person, the false-positive rate at z = 4 moves from 3.2e-05 to 3.3e-05.\nThe worst single key observed gives 1.3e-04 on model text: one in 7,700 rather\nthan one in 31,600.\n\nWhat it does change is the precision of the promise. The published false-positive\nrate is per-key approximate rather than exact, and a deployment needing a hard\nbound should measure its own key against in-domain text instead of trusting the\nnominal figure. That is now stated rather than implied away.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
